ar8030: unlock before the early-exit break in bb_read_thread

bb_read_thread()'s loop locks handle_lk, checks bg_running_flg, and --
if it's already false -- breaks out of the loop while still holding
that lock. The code immediately after the loop re-locks the same,
non-recursive handle_lk. If this early-exit path is ever taken, the
thread self-deadlocks on its own already-held mutex and never returns:
never frees its receive buffer, never closes its socket, never exits.

bg_running_flg is exactly what bs_trig_close() (see the sibling fix
closing a leak in create_new_cb()'s subscribe-timeout path) flips to 0
from another thread to ask this reader thread to stop. The common case
-- this thread already blocked inside recv() when that happens -- is
fine: closing the socket makes recv() return 0, which hits a different,
already-unlocked break further down. But if bg_running_flg goes false
in the narrow window between this thread's own read cycles, right as
it re-checks the flag at the top of the loop, it hits this early-exit
path instead and deadlocks itself, permanently leaking the connection
and its thread.

diff --git a/app/ar8030/ar8030.c b/app/ar8030/ar8030.c
index 3a19b7d..65d7749 100644
--- a/app/ar8030/ar8030.c
+++ b/app/ar8030/ar8030.c
@@ -61,6 +61,17 @@ void* bb_read_thread(void* p)

         pthread_mutex_lock(&phandle->handle_lk);
         if (!phandle->bg_running_flg) {
+            /* Must unlock before breaking: the code right after this loop
+             * (line ~131) re-locks this same non-recursive handle_lk, and
+             * this exact check is what bs_trig_close() flips false from
+             * another thread -- if that happens to land exactly here
+             * (rather than while this thread is blocked in the recv()
+             * below, the far more common case, where closing the socket
+             * just makes recv() return 0 and hit a different, already-
+             * unlocked break path), the thread would self-deadlock on its
+             * own already-held mutex and never exit, permanently leaking
+             * this connection and its thread. */
+            pthread_mutex_unlock(&phandle->handle_lk);
             break;
         }
         pthread_mutex_unlock(&phandle->handle_lk);
